!deck: Math::Functions
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Function Asymptote

!id: 3HvAcxaw5c
!front: Define a vertical asymptote of \( f \) at \( x=a \).
!back:
The line \( x=a \) is a vertical asymptote of \( f \) if at least one of the one-sided limits

- \( \lim_{x\to a^-} f(x) \) or
- \( \lim_{x\to a^+} f(x) \)

is \( +\infty \) or \( -\infty \).
!extra: ![Vertical asymptote x = 2 for f(x) = 1/(x-2)](img/asy_v.svg)
~~~

!id: zzYdjtgp1r
!front: Define a horizontal asymptote of \( f \).
!back:
The line \( y=L \) is a horizontal asymptote of \( f \) if

- \( \lim_{x\to\infty} f(x) = L \) or
- \( \lim_{x\to-\infty} f(x) = L \).
!extra:
A function can have different horizontal asymptotes (or none at all) in the two directions.

![Horizontal asymptotes y = ±π/2 for f(x) = arctan(x)](img/asy_h.svg)
~~~

!id: qB9L6UhrH4
!front: Define an oblique (slant) asymptote of \( f \).
!back:
The line \( y=mx+b \), with \( m \ne 0 \), is an oblique asymptote of \( f \) as \( x\to\infty \) if
\[ \lim_{x\to\infty} \big(f(x) - (mx+b)\big) = 0, \]
and similarly as \( x \to -\infty \).
!extra: ![Oblique asymptote y = x for f(x) = (x^2+1)/x](img/asy_o.svg)
~~~

!id: Sv1uQRdxot
!front: Compare vertical, horizontal, and oblique asymptotes.
!back:
- Vertical asymptote \( x=a \): \( f \) grows without bound (\( \to \pm\infty \)) as \( x \) approaches the finite point \( a \).
- Horizontal asymptote \( y=L \): \( f \) tends to a constant \( L \) as \( x \to \pm\infty \).
- Oblique asymptote \( y=mx+b \): \( f \) tends to a nonconstant linear function as \( x \to \pm\infty \).
~~~

!id: EUyF0kd7hf
!front: Can the graph of \( f \) cross its own horizontal asymptote?
!back:
Yes — a horizontal asymptote only describes end behavior as \( x\to\pm\infty \), not that the graph avoids the line elsewhere.

For \( f(x) = \dfrac{\sin x}{x} \), \( \lim_{x\to\infty} f(x) = 0 \), so \( y=0 \) is a horizontal asymptote, yet \( f(x)=0 \) infinitely often.

![sin(x)/x crossing its horizontal asymptote y = 0 at every nonzero multiple of pi](img/asy_cross.svg)
~~~

!id: oqdDQ8l5Gm
!front: Can a function have different oblique asymptotes as \( x\to\infty \) and as \( x\to-\infty \)? Give an example.
!back:
Yes. For \( f(x) = \sqrt{x^2+1} \):
- As \( x\to\infty \): \( f(x) - x = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+1}+x} \to 0 \), so \( y=x \) is an asymptote.
- As \( x\to-\infty \): \( f(x)-(-x) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+1}-x} \to 0 \), so \( y=-x \) is an asymptote.

![sqrt(x^2+1) approaching y = x on the right and y = -x on the left](img/asy_two_sided.svg)
~~~

!id: xWvUGS6lr7
!front: Find all asymptotes of \( f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-2} \).
!back:
- Vertical asymptote \( x=2 \) (denominator zero, no cancellation);
- horizontal asymptote \( y=0 \) (numerator has lower degree than the denominator).
- No oblique asymptote.

![1/(x-2) with both its vertical asymptote x = 2 and horizontal asymptote y = 0 labeled](img/asy_v_and_h.svg)
~~~

!id: hTjCFmOHhv
!front: Find all asymptotes of \( f(x) = \dfrac{x^2+1}{x} \).
!back:
- Vertical asymptote \( x=0 \). Dividing gives \( f(x) = x + \dfrac{1}{x} \), and \( 1/x \to 0 \) as \( x\to\pm\infty \), so \( y=x \) is an oblique asymptote.
- No horizontal asymptote, since the numerator's degree exceeds the denominator's.

![(x^2+1)/x with both its vertical asymptote x = 0 and oblique asymptote y = x labeled](img/asy_v_and_o.svg)
~~~

!id: uW5iBbeAze
!front: Find all asymptotes of \( f(x) = \dfrac{x^2-1}{x-1} \).
!back:
None. The factor \( x-1 \) cancels: \( f(x) = x+1 \) for \( x\ne 1 \), so \( x=1 \) is a removable discontinuity (a hole), not a vertical asymptote. The simplified function is itself a line, so it has no horizontal or oblique asymptote either — it doesn't approach a different line at infinity, it is one.

![(x^2-1)/(x-1) simplifying to the line y = x+1 with a removable hole at (1,2), no asymptote](img/asy_none.svg)
~~~
